<Guide
  lang="en"
  route="hand16v10"
  title="16 vs 10 in Blackjack: Hit or Stand? (The Math)"
  h1="16 vs 10: hit or stand?"
  description="What to do with 16 against a dealer 10 in blackjack: hit, stand or surrender. The exact expected value of each option, why hitting is better and the exceptions."
  updated="2026-09-27"
  readMinutes={5}
  faq={faq}
  related={['strategy', 'whenDouble', 'course', 'dataBust']}
>
  <div slot="top">
    <div class="tldr"><p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> with hard 16 against a 10, <strong>surrender if the table allows it; otherwise, hit</strong>. It’s the most dreaded hand in blackjack because every option loses, but hitting loses a little less than standing. The exception: if your 16 is 8-8, split (at hole-card tables).</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="numbers">The exact numbers</h2>
  <p>Here’s what each option is worth on average, per unit bet. They’re all negative: with 16 against a 10 you’re going to lose money whatever you do. The question is which option loses the least.</p>
  <EVTable lang="en" cards={[10, 6]} up={10} tables={['vegasS17', 'doubleDeck', 'spainCasino']} />
  <p>Under Las Vegas rules, <strong>surrender</strong> (−0.500) is best: you get half your bet back and skip a hand that loses more than half the time. Without surrender, <strong>hitting</strong> (−0.535) beats standing (−0.542).</p>
  <p>At a European-style table with no hole card, such as in Spanish casinos, the losses are a bit bigger because the dealer can still turn out to have blackjack, but the order doesn’t change: hitting is still better.</p>

  <h2 id="why">Why hit when you bust so often</h2>
  <p>Intuition says, “with 16 I’m almost sure to bust, so I’ll stand.” But standing on 16 is good for only one thing: waiting for the dealer to bust. And with a 10 showing, the dealer busts only about <strong>23%</strong> of the time (see the <a href={routes.dataBust.en}>dealer bust probability table</a>). The other 77% of the time the dealer ends on 17 or more, and your 16 loses.</p>
  <p>If you hit, you bust when a 6, 7, 8, 9 or 10 comes (roughly 6 times out of 10). But when an ace, 2, 3, 4 or 5 comes, you improve to 17–21 and have a real chance to win or push. Those wins more than make up for the small number of extra hands you lose by busting.</p>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>The difference is small, and that’s why people argue about it so much.</strong> Hitting instead of standing saves you less than one hundredth of a bet per hand. You won’t notice it in a single hand. Over a thousand hands of 16 vs 10, it adds up to seven whole bets.</p></div>

  <h2 id="exceptions">The exceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>8-8:</strong> split. Two hands starting on 8 lose quite a bit less than one 16. At European no-hole-card tables, 8-8 against a 10 is a hit (or surrender, if it’s allowed).</li>
    <li><strong>A 16 made of three or more cards</strong> (for example 4-5-7): standing becomes equal or better, because the low cards that would help you are already in your hand.</li>
    <li><strong>If you count cards:</strong> at a true count of 0 or higher, stand. It’s the most famous index play in Hi-Lo: when lots of high cards are left, hitting busts you too often.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="other">What about 16 against other upcards?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Against 2 through 6:</strong> stand. The dealer is weak and will bust often.</li>
    <li><strong>Against 7 or 8:</strong> hit.</li>
    <li><strong>Against 9 or ace:</strong> surrender if you can; otherwise, hit.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>It’s all in the <a href={routes.strategy.en}>basic strategy chart</a>, which you can adjust to your table’s rules.</p>
</Guide>
